# Target C-II — one adjacent O--O pair is also metrically close along the center train

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Status:** proof-only finite/source-level structural strengthening  
**Scope:** full-core Type-II branch with `t=(q-k)/D<=1.844`; after the current exclusion this is live on `1.843651<t<=1.844`  
**Dependencies:** audited triple-small adjacent-cut theorem; audited finite train geometry; exact laminar O--O interval rows; audited Section 157 KKT resultants  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_adjacent_cut_metric_quadruple_small.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The current triple-small theorem forces one consecutive smallest-leaf O--O cut pair
with three simultaneous small quantities:

- only `O(1)` switching actual diameter rows;
- switching KKT multiplier mass `O(D^{-1})`; and
- projective leaf-direction gap `O(D^{-1})`.

That theorem is topological/algebraic.  The chosen pair could still be far apart in the
actual source.  The same averaging argument has one unused metric budget: all selected
leaf cuts are crossed by one common center train, and the **total arclength of that
center train is less than `2D/sqrt(3)`**.

Adding this fourth budget gives a pair that is simultaneously finite-cusp and
physically close.

### Theorem MQ — metric quadruple-small adjacent pair

For every unbounded survivor sequence in the stated `t<=1.844` scope, for all
sufficiently large members there are consecutive smallest-leaf outer-to-outer trains

\[
\tau_i,\tau_{i+1}
\]

on the good-leaf backbone from the triple-small construction, crossed in that order by
the same center train `gamma`, such that the following hold simultaneously.

Let `u_i,u_{i+1}` be coherently oriented unit contact directions, let

\[
N_i
=\#\{h:\varepsilon_{h,i+1}\ne\varepsilon_{hi}\},
\]

and let

\[
B_i
=\sum_h\beta_h
|\varepsilon_{h,i+1}-\varepsilon_{hi}|.
\]

If `x_i,x_{i+1}` are the centers of the two strict rhombi

\[
\gamma\cap\tau_i,
\qquad
\gamma\cap\tau_{i+1},
\]

and `ell_i` is the arclength of the `gamma` centerline subarc between them, then

\[
\boxed{N_i<54000+o(1),}
\tag{MQ.1}
\]

\[
\boxed{B_i<{54000\over D}+o(D^{-1}),}
\tag{MQ.2}
\]

\[
\boxed{
d_{\mathbb{RP}^1}(u_i,u_{i+1})
<{54000\over D}+o(D^{-1}),
}
\tag{MQ.3}
\]

\[
\boxed{
ell_i<540+o(1).}
\tag{MQ.4}
\]

In particular

\[
\boxed{|x_{i+1}-x_i|<540+o(1).}
\tag{MQ.5}
\]

The Section-157 cut resultants also satisfy

\[
\boxed{
\|F_{i+1}-F_i\|<54000+o(1),
\qquad
|A_{i+1}-A_i|<54000+o(1).
}
\tag{MQ.6}

The constants are deliberately coarse.  The exact combined average is below
`53575`, and the corresponding arclength average is below `535.75`.

The new information is the scale in (MQ.4): the two finite-cusp cuts are not only
adjacent in the complement dual; their actual crossings with the common center train
are separated by a **uniformly bounded physical path length independent of `D`**.

---

## 1. Reuse the exact triple-small backbone

Use exactly the notation and parameter choice of the hostile-audited triple-small
adjacent-cut theorem.  It fixes

\[
\lambda={677\over1000},
\qquad
r={61\over125},
\]

and extracts one center-phase train `gamma` crossing, in order, a path

\[
P=(\tau_1,\ldots,\tau_m)
\]

of distinct good smallest-leaf O--O trains with

\[
\boxed{
m>pD-o(D),
}
\tag{1.1}
\]

where

\[
p={278778993\over78216868750}
=0.0035641799199\ldots .
\tag{1.2}
\]

For the real phase offset of leaf `tau_j` from `gamma`, write as before

\[
a_j
:=\beta_j-\alpha_\gamma-\frac\pi3>0.
\tag{1.3}
\]

The lower center-neighbor density is

\[
d_{low}
={9891\over62500}
=0.158256.
\tag{1.4}
\]

The global strict-rhombus angular-defect budget then gives

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{j=1}^m a_j
\le {C_{phase}\over d_{low}}+o(1),
}
\tag{1.5}
\]

where

\[
C_{phase}
={\pi^2\sqrt3(2+\sqrt3)\over12}.
\tag{1.6}
\]

For adjacent selected leaves,

\[
d_{\mathbb{RP}^1}(u_i,u_{i+1})
=|a_{i+1}-a_i|
\le a_i+a_{i+1}.
\tag{1.7}
\]

The interval-row theorem supplies the two global switch budgets

\[
\sum_{i=1}^{m-1}N_i\le2\pi D+o(D),
\tag{1.8}
\]

\[
\sum_{i=1}^{m-1}B_i\le2+o(1).
\tag{1.9}
\]

These are exactly the three budgets used by the preceding theorem.

---

## 2. Every complete train has arclength less than `2D/sqrt(3)`

This is the new metric input.

Fix any complete stopped train `gamma` and orient its unit contact-edge direction by a
unit vector `u`.  Inside one strict unit rhombus traversed by `gamma`, the train
centerline joins the midpoints of the two opposite `u`-edges.  That segment is a
translate of the other unit side vector of the rhombus.  Hence its Euclidean length is
exactly one.

Orient the centerline so the scalar train height

\[
h_u(p)=\det(u,p)
\]

increases.  Triangle-freeness puts the oriented side-angle strictly between `pi/3`
and `2pi/3`.  Therefore every complete centerline segment advances in height by

\[
\Delta h_u
=|\det(u,v)|
>\frac{\sqrt3}{2}.
\tag{2.1}
\]

Every endpoint of every centerline segment is the midpoint of a source contact edge,
so it lies in `conv(A)`.  The diameter of `conv(A)` equals the source diameter `D`, and
`h_u` is a unit-Lipschitz linear functional.  Thus the total `h_u` range available to
the whole train is at most `D`.

If `R_\gamma` is the number of strict rhombi traversed by the train, summing (2.1)
gives

\[
R_\gamma{\sqrt3\over2}<D.
\]

Hence

\[
\boxed{
R_\gamma<{2D\over\sqrt3}.
}
\tag{2.2}

Since each centerline segment has length one, the entire centerline arclength is
exactly `R_gamma`.  Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{len}(\gamma)<{2D\over\sqrt3}.
}
\tag{2.3}

No near-disk assumption, phase estimate, or KKT weight is used here.

---

## 3. The selected crossing gaps spend one common arclength budget

Let `x_j` be the center of the crossing rhombus `gamma cap tau_j`.  Because the leaves
are listed in the order traversed by `gamma`, these points occur in the same order
along the oriented centerline.

Define

\[
\ell_i
:=\operatorname{len}_\gamma[x_i,x_{i+1}]
\qquad(1\le i<m).
\tag{3.1}
\]

The subarcs have disjoint interiors and concatenate from `x_1` to `x_m`.  Therefore

\[
\sum_{i=1}^{m-1}\ell_i
\le\operatorname{len}(\gamma)
<{2D\over\sqrt3}.
\tag{3.2}

Also Euclidean chord length is at most path length:

\[
|x_{i+1}-x_i|\le\ell_i.
\tag{3.3}

This fourth global budget is independent of the phase/switching budgets above.

---

## 4. Average all four costs on the same boundary

For each internal boundary put

\[
T_i
:=N_i
+DB_i
+D(a_i+a_{i+1})
+100\ell_i.
\tag{4.1}
\]

Every summand is nonnegative.  Equations (1.5), (1.8), (1.9), and (3.2) give

\[
\begin{aligned}
\sum_{i=1}^{m-1}T_i
&\le
\left(
2\pi+2
+{2C_{phase}\over d_{low}}
+{200\over\sqrt3}
\right)D
+o(D).
\end{aligned}
\tag{4.2}

Using `m>pD-o(D)`, some internal boundary obeys

\[
T_i
\le
{2\pi+2+2C_{phase}/d_{low}+200/\sqrt3\over p}
+o(1).
\tag{4.3}

The exact certificate uses the outward rational bounds

\[
\pi<{355\over113},
\qquad
\sqrt3<{17321\over10000},
\qquad
\sqrt3>{433\over250},
\]

and proves

\[
\boxed{
{2\pi+2+2C_{phase}/d_{low}+200/\sqrt3\over p}
<53575<54000.
}
\tag{4.4}

Since all four terms in (4.1) are nonnegative, the first three are each below
`54000+o(1)`, while

\[
100\ell_i<54000+o(1),
\]

so

\[
\ell_i<540+o(1).
\]

Equations (MQ.1)--(MQ.4) follow, and (3.3) gives (MQ.5).

Finally, the rooted Section-157 resultant identity gives

\[
\|F_{i+1}-F_i\|\le DB_i,
\]

and reverse triangle inequality gives

\[
|A_{i+1}-A_i|\le\|F_{i+1}-F_i\|,
\]

which proves (MQ.6).

---

## 5. Finite-cusp interpretation

Choose `s in {+1,-1}` so that the ordinary angle between `u_i` and `s u_{i+1}` is the
projective gap.  Then

\[
|u_i-su_{i+1}|
\le d_{\mathbb{RP}^1}(u_i,u_{i+1})
<{54000\over D}+o(D^{-1}).
\tag{5.1}

For the exact opposite two-cut multishear with coefficients `(1,-s)`, every diameter
row spanning both cuts therefore has first relative-displacement coefficient
`O(D^{-1})`.  Only the switching set can retain a unit-scale coefficient, and by
(MQ.1)--(MQ.2) that switching set has bounded cardinality and total KKT multiplier
`O(D^{-1})`.

The previous finite-cusp theorem already gave this normal form with the sharper
constant `21200`.  The present theorem trades part of that constant for the new metric
fact

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{len}_\gamma[x_i,x_{i+1}]<540+o(1).
}
\]

Thus the exceptional diameter cusps are now tied to two phase-close O--O cuts whose
actual crossing rhombi with one common center train lie in a uniformly bounded
transversal corridor.

This still does **not** give a first-order feasible diameter descent: positive KKT
stationarity and mixed rigidity prohibit any nonzero componentwise nonincreasing
train-shear direction.  The point is to localize the finite obstruction geometrically
for a nonlocal replacement or contact-breaking argument.

---

## 6. Why the metric addition matters

Before this theorem, “adjacent” meant only consecutive edges of a complement-dual
path.  A branch region could be geometrically large, so dual adjacency alone supplied
no source-scale metric control.

The selected pair now has an actual source path joining its two grid crossings whose
length is bounded independently of `D`.  Any closing argument may therefore work in a
fixed-radius neighborhood of that transversal while the global source diameter tends
to infinity.

Promising uses are:

1. compare the bounded switching diameter-chord set with the two nearby crossing
   rhombi and the common-center contact chain;
2. allow a controlled finite number of contacts in this bounded corridor to break,
   rather than insisting on the locally jammed exact-contact shear space; or
3. combine the bounded transversal with the diameter-thrackle / interval-support
   geometry to constrain where switching loaded endpoints can occur.

No such closing implication is claimed here.

---

## 7. Scope audit

- The path `P` and center train `gamma` are exactly those already certified by the
  triple-small theorem; no new rectangle extraction is assumed.
- The centerline-length bound uses only exact unit-rhombus geometry and the global
  diameter `D`.
- Crossing points `x_j` are rhombus centers, so they lie on the actual train centerline;
  they need not be source vertices.
- The `540` bound is on centerline arclength and therefore also bounds Euclidean
  separation, but it does not bound the diameter of the entire dual strip between the
  two O--O cuts.
- The theorem intentionally weakens `21200` to `54000` to obtain a bounded physical
  transversal on the same pair.
- No KKT coefficient lower bound, response theorem, or local diameter descent is used.

**Disposition:** proved proof-only metric quadruple-small adjacent-pair theorem. C-II
and Level I remain open.
